---
name: video-making
description: Produce videos programmatically - slideshows, explainers, animated charts, title cards, captioned clips, GIFs and simple motion graphics - using ffmpeg, Pillow and headless-browser frame rendering. Use whenever the user asks to make, edit, trim, merge, resize, subtitle, speed up, add music to or convert a video or animated GIF, or turn images/slides/text into a video.
---

# Making video in the sandbox

## Toolbox

- **ffmpeg / ffprobe**: encode, trim, concatenate, scale, overlay, subtitles, audio mixing, GIFs.
- **Pillow**: draw frames (title cards, captions, charts you render yourself).
- **Playwright**: render HTML/CSS/JS animations to frames by stepping time and taking screenshots.
- **matplotlib**: animated charts via saved frames.
- Check what is installed first (`ffmpeg -version`, `python -c "import PIL"`). Encoder availability varies (`ffmpeg -encoders | grep x264`).

## Workflow

1. **Brief**: goal, length (keep it as short as the message allows), aspect ratio (16:9 for desktop, 9:16 for phone-first, 1:1 for feeds), resolution (1280x720 is plenty for previews), frame rate (24–30 fps), audio yes/no.
2. **Storyboard**: a table of scenes with duration, visual, on-screen text, narration/music. Show it to the user for anything beyond a trivial clip.
3. **Assets**: gather or generate images, text, audio. Only use media the user provided or that you generated; do not use copyrighted clips or music.
4. **Build frames or clips**: one scene at a time; keep each scene reproducible by script.
5. **Assemble** with ffmpeg; mix audio; add fades.
6. **Check**: `ffprobe` duration/streams, extract a few frames to PNG at start/middle/end and look at them, check the audio levels if present.
7. Save to `/mnt/user-data/outputs/` and present.

## Design rules for on-screen video

- Text: large (>= 5% of frame height), high contrast, safe margins (>= 5% each side), max ~12 words per screen, hold each text at least `0.3 s + 0.06 s × words`.
- Motion: ease in/out, 0.3–0.6 s transitions, no more than one thing moving at once.
- Consistency: one font family, 2–3 colors, same layout grid.
- Pacing: new visual every 3–7 s for explainers.
- Accessibility: captions for anything with speech; do not rely on color alone.

## Reliable ffmpeg recipes

```bash
# Images -> video (each shown 3 s, 1280x720, letterboxed, H.264, yuv420p for broad compatibility)
ffmpeg -framerate 1/3 -pattern_type glob -i 'frames/*.png' \
  -vf "scale=1280:720:force_original_aspect_ratio=decrease,pad=1280:720:(ow-iw)/2:(oh-ih)/2,format=yuv420p" \
  -r 30 -c:v libx264 -crf 20 out.mp4

# Trim without re-encoding (cut at keyframes) / precise trim (re-encode)
ffmpeg -ss 00:00:05 -to 00:00:12 -i in.mp4 -c copy cut.mp4
ffmpeg -ss 00:00:05 -to 00:00:12 -i in.mp4 -c:v libx264 -c:a aac cut.mp4

# Concatenate files with identical codecs
printf "file '%s'\n" a.mp4 b.mp4 > list.txt && ffmpeg -f concat -safe 0 -i list.txt -c copy joined.mp4

# Add audio, stop at the shorter stream
ffmpeg -i video.mp4 -i music.mp3 -map 0:v -map 1:a -c:v copy -c:a aac -shortest out.mp4

# Burn in subtitles from an .srt
ffmpeg -i in.mp4 -vf "subtitles=subs.srt" -c:a copy out.mp4

# Speed up 2x (video + audio)
ffmpeg -i in.mp4 -filter_complex "[0:v]setpts=0.5*PTS[v];[0:a]atempo=2.0[a]" -map "[v]" -map "[a]" out.mp4

# High-quality GIF
ffmpeg -i in.mp4 -vf "fps=12,scale=640:-1:flags=lanczos,split[a][b];[a]palettegen[p];[b][p]paletteuse" out.gif

# Fade in/out
ffmpeg -i in.mp4 -vf "fade=t=in:st=0:d=0.5,fade=t=out:st=4.5:d=0.5" out.mp4
```

Always use `-pix_fmt yuv420p` (or `format=yuv420p`) for H.264 so QuickTime and phones can play it. Quote paths with spaces.

## Scripts

`scripts/make_slideshow.py` builds a captioned slideshow from a JSON storyboard (images or plain color cards, captions, durations, optional audio) with cross-fades:

```
python scripts/make_slideshow.py storyboard.json out.mp4
```

Storyboard format:

```json
{"size": [1280, 720], "fps": 30, "background": "#101820", "font_size": 54,
 "scenes": [
   {"text": "Welcome", "seconds": 3},
   {"image": "photo.jpg", "text": "A caption", "seconds": 4}
 ],
 "audio": "music.mp3"}
```

## Pitfalls

- Odd dimensions break H.264: use even width/height.
- Variable-frame-rate phone clips drift against audio: normalize with `-r 30` (and `-vsync cfr` on older ffmpeg).
- Re-encoding repeatedly degrades quality: do edits in one pass when possible.
- Large outputs: a 60 s 720p clip at CRF 23 is a few MB; check size before presenting, and offer a smaller version if needed.
